le feeds E005 but is transitively ordered via E003, and the summary explicitly justifies the non-edge.\n41\t\n42\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n43\tComponent/team tuples produce separate epics: E003 (kserve midstream) and E004 (odh-model-controller) share the canonical component \"INFERENG Midstream\" (both names verified in `.context/rhai-components.txt`) but are different repos and different logical changes — correctly separate epics with the mapping logged in the summary. E002's `llm-d` component is a valid canonical name (EPP belongs to llm-d) and is a single-team Investigation. E005 is Documentation/Documentation team. No scope duplication: E002 produces validated trigger configurations that E005 consumes (producer/consumer, not duplicate build). No work-product type mixing — code and content authoring are in separate epics. Minor observation (not score-reducing): E003 bundles the full llmisvc-controller work stream (upstream integration, API types, CEL validation, controller branching, phase-specific scaling, status conditions) that the strategy estimates at 1-2 sprints — at or slightly above the ~2-week epic guideline. Acceptable given the upstream reference implementation (kserve/kserve#5839) delivers most of the code, and splitting the CRD API from its controller would create artificial boundaries, but it is the largest epic in the set.\n44\t\n45\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n46\tE001 and E002 are genuine Investigations: their outcomes change what E003 does (rewrite scope for adaptation findings; add remediation for failed scale-to/from-zero validation), and both have E003 as a downstream dependent. E003/E004/E005 are Implementations producing concrete artifacts (controller code, reconciler code, docs). E003's `gated_by` (E001, E002) references only epics in its direct `dependencies`, and `gate_failure_impact` has both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated — the fallback text covers all three gate failure modes (adaptation rewrite, validation remediation, upstream-delay carry-patch). No epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by`. Minor observation (not score-reducing): the single `gate_failure_impact.action: rewrite` cannot express that the E002 gate was classified `add_remediation` in the summary (the schema permits one action value); the fallback_approach text covers both cases, so execution is unaffected.\n47\t\n48\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n49\tSignal sets are type-appropriate: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals`, E003/E004/E005 carry `ai_signals`; all five ai-signals rationale files exist and match the frontmatter values. E001's and E002's investigation signals are well justified against the 5-signal rubric (notably E002's `source_accessibility: 0` for runtime reliability questions and `cluster_hardware_dependence: -2` for a gating question requiring a live cluster — both correct applications of the rubric). E003's signals correctly penalize the two gating investigations and the upstream dependency. One arguable value: E005's `human_process_gates: 0`. The strategy's Prerequisites & Process Gates lists \"Documentation Support: Pending human review\" — a strategy-level gate that applies directly to the docs epic — and E003/E004 score `-1` citing the analogous pending strategy gates (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding). The rationale defends 0 by conflating the Rule 24 technical-review AC (review of the deliverable, standard docs workflow) with the pending strategy-level process gate. Defensible either way, but inconsistent with the treatment of the sibling Implementation epics; `human_process_gates: -1` would be more consistent. No arithmetic or threshold checking performed (pipeline-computed).\n50\t\n51\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n52\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy: E003 reproduces all seven strategy acceptance criteria plus backwards-compatibility regression ACs and the upstream-delay fallback AC; E004's ACs cover resource creation, authenticated query success, owner-reference cleanup, and no-regression against the v1beta1 flow; E002's ACs demand pass/fail with evidence; E001's ACs demand a traceable adaptation list and recommendation. Rule-mandated ACs: Rule 24 (docs technically reviewed against implementation) is present on E005; Rule 23 (rollback/feature-flag) is not applicable — the change is additive, not a replacement, and regression ACs are present anyway; Rule 25 is not applicable (no konflux chain). The metric-outage scope trap flagged in the strategy is handled as a confirm-and-configure AC on E003 rather than a new epic — correct handling. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by scanning all five bodies).\n53\t\n54\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n55\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: two autoscaling paths with API/validation/controller changes (E003), downstream TriggerAuthentication flow (E004), scale-to/from-zero and disaggregated scaling (E002 validation + E003 implementation), and the full documentation surface — decision guide covering WVA/standalone KEDA/static allocation, configuration recipes, supported configuration matrix, troubleshooting, KEDA prerequisite guidance (overlay 0008 / ODH-ADR-0007), TriggerAuthentication operator instructions, cold-start expectations, and Prometheus capacity guidance (E005). All three strategy open questions are explicitly dispositioned in the Investigation Scoping Notes (two as Investigations, one pair as doc content inputs with ACs). Risks are carried forward: upstream PR delay (E001 + E003 fallback AC), scale-from-zero trigger misconfiguration (E002 + E005), cold-start latency (E005), KEDA dependency (E005), API surface complexity (E005 decision guide). NFRs map to epic scope (backwards compatibility → E003 ACs; security → E004; observability → E003/E005; scalability → E005). A `docs-authoring` epic exists (required by HLR-7 and the Documentation affected component) even though \"Documentation Support\" is listed as pending rather than \"Yes\". No conditional branches are needed (the upstream-PR uncertainty has a sanctioned fallback, correctly handled per Rule 21 as an AC rather than a separate epic). No dropped strategy-level context found.\n56\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94d15d6c88434dd1","agent_id":"af1dd8dd1a4ec2804","session_id":"a7d955e8-9b81-49a1-9fe3-77d8218dfc96","uuid":"272ff8ef-c3bf-4ef6-ba4f-2614698689a1","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:11:49.894Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-996ae82e2eb58632","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
